 ## Overview
-Spherical Harmonics (SH) Approximation is a technique to represent a spherical function $f(\omega)$ using a finite subset of the SH basis functions, enabling efficient and differentiable computation for inverse problems in volume rendering [S1]. It solves the problem of expensive Monte Carlo integration by projecting directional data (such as lighting, visibility, and in-scattering) into the SH domain, where integrals become simple dot products and convolutions become matrix-vector multiplications [S1]. Unlike its sibling [Spherical Harmonics Degree Selection](10564-spherical-harmonics-degree-selection.md), which determines the optimal bandwidth, this method focuses on the projection and reconstruction mechanics using a fixed bandwidth [S1].
+Spherical Harmonics (SH) Approximation is a technique to represent a spherical function $f(\omega)$ using a finite subset of the SH basis functions, enabling efficient and differentiable computation for inverse problems in volume rendering [S1]. It solves the problem of expensive Monte Carlo integration by projecting directional data (such as lighting, visibility, and in-scattering) into the SH domain, where integrals become simple dot products and convolutions become matrix-vector multiplications [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A spherical function $f(\omega)$ defined over the domain $\Omega$ (the unit sphere), and a maximum bandwidth $L$ (or number of bands $n$).
-**Outputs:** A vector of SH coefficients $\mathbf{f}$ of size $n = (L+1)^2$.
